[00:11.02]‘How do you get to school?’
[00:13.52]This question often gets an answer like ‘By bus’ or ‘I walk’ or ‘My parents take me by car’.
[00:22.80]But not always – there are children in many different parts of the world who, every day, have to go on a difficult journey in order to get to their lessons.
[00:34.62]They travel for kilometres on foot, or by boat, bicycle, donkey or train.
[00:42.64]They cross deserts, mountains, rivers, snow and ice: for example, the children of the Iñupiat community in Alaska go to school and then come back when it is dark, in extremely cold temperatures.
[01:02.04]And they are not the only ones.
[01:04.44]Kids in many countries do this and more.
[01:09.70]These children in Indonesia have to cross a bridge ten metres above a dangerous river to get to their class on time.
[01:19.20](Some years ago the bridge fell down after very heavy rain.)
[01:25.52]Then they walk many more kilometres through the forest to their school in Banten.
[01:34.14]A pupil at Gulu Village Primary School, China, rides a donkey as his grandfather walks beside him.
[01:44.00]Gulu is a mountain village in a national park.
[01:48.24]The school is far away from the village.
[01:51.60]It is halfway up a mountain, so it takes five hours to climb from the bottom of the mountain to the school.
[02:01.34]The children have a dangerous journey: the path is only 45 centimetres wide in some places.
[02:10.68]In Sri Lanka, some children have to cross a piece of wood between two walls of an old castle every morning.
[02:18.90]Their teacher watches them carefully.
[02:23.10]But in Galle, Sri Lanka, many girls don’t go to school – they have to go to work or get married young.
[02:32.07]So girls are happy to take a risk in order to get to school.
[02:40.02]Six-year-old Fabricio Oliveira gets on his donkey every morning to ride with his friends for over an hour through a desert region in the very dry Sertão area of northeast Brazil.
[02:56.40]Their school is in Extrema.
[02:59.12]It’s a tiny village – very few people live there.
[03:03.84]These children live in houses on Chetla Road in Delhi, India.
[03:10.10]Their homes are near the busy and dangerous railway lines that go to Alipur station.
[03:17.36]Every morning they walk along the tracks to get to their school, 40 minutes away.
[03:24.18]So one question we can ask is: why do the children do this?
[03:30.56]Because their parents make them do it?
[03:33.68]The answer, in many cases, is no – it’s because for them going to school means a better future: they hope to get a job and money so they can help their families and their neighbours.
[03:49.86]And this is why rivers, deserts or danger won’t stop them on their way to school.
